"""Gallery for the cezanne: the photos and videos of the home directory, as
the Android gallery shows them. Photos: a timeline (newest first, one header
per day) of the media folders: the camera, screenshots, screen recordings,
Pictures, Videos and Downloads. Albums: one per folder; the camera's photos
(~/Pictures/Camera) and videos (~/Videos/Camera) are one album, as DCIM is.
Folders elsewhere in the home directory (projects, game data) are under
"Other folders"; each can be added to Photos or hidden. The viewer swipes
between items, zooms (pinch, double tap), plays videos, shows the details and
moves items to the trash with an undo.

What is a photo or a video and where it lies comes from GNOME's file indexer
(LocalSearch; SPARQL over D-Bus with TinySPARQL). It indexes $HOME
recursively, skips hidden directories and every folder holding a .nomedia
file (Android's convention: "Hide folder" writes one), and its notifier
brings in new files, such as Snapshot's pictures, while the gallery is open.
Thumbnails are the freedesktop cache (~/.cache/thumbnails) filled by
gnome-desktop's factory and the thumbnailers (glycin for images,
ffmpegthumbnailer for videos). Full images load through glycin, which
applies the orientation. The camera's RAW pairs (same name, .dng and .jpeg or
.png) show as one picture; a DNG without its JPEG shows the preview embedded
in it (gexiv2). Videos play through a playbin of their own (VideoPlayer):
decoded in hardware, converted in system memory for gtk4paintablesink.
"Upscale 2x (AI)" writes an upscaled copy next to a photo with cezanne-photo
(Real-ESRGAN x4plus on the APU through cezanne-upscaled, else on the GPU) or
a video with cezanne-video-upscale (the compact Real-ESRGAN on the APU, up
to 1080p), in the background, one at a time.
"""
